Title:
PhantomCtx: New Approach to Activation Context Hijacking for EDR Evasion

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post introduces PhantomCtx, a Windows user-mode loader that automates Activation Context (SxS) hijacking to redirect DLL resolution and load an arbitrary payload DLL into signed executables.  
- It targets the SxS manifest redirection stage of the Windows DLL search order, avoiding the need for a specifically “vulnerable” signed binary typically required for classic DLL hijacking/sideloading.  
- The author details Activation Context internals (TEB/PEB pointers, ToC sections, DLL redirection section Id==2) and how to patch or craft redirection entries to hijack IAT-imported DLLs.  
- A first implementation using remote memory allocation/writes is shown to trigger Elastic XDR detections focused on cross-process `NtWriteVirtualMemory`, especially against suspended processes and trusted targets.  
- The key contribution is a stealthier technique: replacing the read-only Activation Context mapping by unmapping the original view and remapping a pagefile-backed section at the same address, eliminating remote memory writes while keeping `PEB.ActivationContextData` valid.  
- Useful for red teams and offensive security researchers interested in user-mode tradecraft and EDR-aware process manipulation; also relevant to defenders for understanding a less common injection primitive.

Technical Focus:
- Windows SxS subsystem (assemblies, manifests) and Activation Contexts
- PEB/TEB Activation Context pointers and loader resolution behavior
- DLL redirection section (Activation Context ToC, Id==2) entry patching/crafting
- Suspended process manipulation without `NtWriteVirtualMemory`
- Section/view primitives: `NtCreateSection`, `NtMapViewOfSection`, `NtUnmapViewOfSection`
- EDR detection logic analysis (Elastic XDR behavioral rules)

Use Cases:
- Load a payload DLL into signed Windows/Microsoft/third-party executables without relying on known sideload candidates
- EDR-evasive user-mode execution by avoiding cross-process memory writes and RWX allocations
- Tradecraft research: alternative to process hollowing / remote thread injection patterns
- Defensive validation: build detections around section remapping/unmapping patterns and Activation Context anomalies

Keywords:
PhantomCtx, Activation Context, SxS, WinSxS, manifest redirection, DLL search order, DLL redirection, IAT imports, PEB.ActivationContextData, TEB.ActivationStack, CreateProcessW suspended, NtReadVirtualMemory, NtWriteVirtualMemory, NtCreateSection, NtMapViewOfSection, NtUnmapViewOfSection, Elastic XDR, EDR evasion, DLL hijacking, sideloading